When small groups of organisms (with small populations) become isolated from their original population (for example, if they migrate across mountain ranges or rivers, or if they move to a new island where they cannot easily return), they often adapt to their new environment in ways significantly different from how they were before.
This new environment offers both different resources and competitors, so new populations will need to possess different traits or adaptability in order to become stronger competitors than what they previously had to face.
The original population numbers have not changed at all, yet they still need to adapt as before.
As time passed, because the new population gradually adapted to their new environment, they began to look increasingly like a different population.
Eventually, after many thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will become so different in characteristics that they can no longer be considered the same species.
We call this process speciation, which refers to the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a crucial part of evolutionary development.
