The Sundarbans are the largest mangrove forest in the world, stretching up to 80 kilometers (50 miles) inland into the borders of Bangladesh and the Indian state of West Bengal.
The Sundarbans have been designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Part of the forest within the Indian territory is known as the National Sundarbans Mangrove Forest.
Forests are not just water sources — they include the last remaining parts of this vast forest that once extended all the way to the Gangetic plains.
The Sundarbans covers an area of 3,850 km², about one-third of which is covered by water/mangrove channels.
The Sundarbans National Park (Sundarbans) has been a protected wildlife reserve since 1966, and it is estimated that the area currently hosts around 400 Royal Bengal tigers and approximately 30,000 other animals.
